WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.700
Disciplined parser flow separates recognizing arguments from executing program behavior.

2
00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:14.600
The parser should collect intent first: which flags are set, which options have values, which positionals were provided, and which combinations are valid.

3
00:00:14.700 --> 00:00:21.900
File operations, resource changes, or expensive work should wait until parsing and validation are complete.

4
00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:24.660
Unknown options should be handled consistently.

5
00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:28.120
A strict tool may reject them with a clear error.

6
00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:31.020
A wrapper may pass some values through by contract.

7
00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:39.440
The important point is that unknown input is not silently interpreted as something else unless that behavior is documented and tested.

8
00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:42.760
Missing option values are a common edge case.

9
00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:53.260
If an option requires a value, the parser should detect when the value is absent, when the next token is another option, or when the value is empty and not allowed.

10
00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:56.560
The usage message should make the expected form clear.

11
00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:01.560
Repeated options should follow the contract established by the interface design.

12
00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:06.060
Reject duplicates, take the final value, or accumulate values intentionally.

13
00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:13.200
Do not let repeated options fall through unrelated branches or overwrite state in a way that later code cannot explain.

14
00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:19.880
Many command-line conventions support an end-of-options marker so later values are treated as positional arguments.

15
00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:23.820
If the tool supports that convention, it should do so deliberately.

16
00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:28.800
If it does not, that choice should be reflected in tests and usage behavior.

17
00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:32.120
Positionals deserve the same care as named options.

18
00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:37.740
Their count, ordering, meaning, and allowed values should be validated before action.

19
00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:44.080
A positional that names a resource or controls a mode is still input crossing the parser boundary.
